UID:ecf-erasmus-40-creative-research-fellowship-2027@artificialnouveau.gith
 ub.io
DTSTAMP:20261104T144744Z
DTSTART;VALUE=DATE:20261201
DTEND;VALUE=DATE:20261202
SUMMARY:Deadline: European Cultural Foundation: ERASMUS 40 Creative Researc
 h Fellowship (Florence archives\, Brussels presentation)
DESCRIPTION:To mark the 40th anniversary of ERASMUS in 2027\, the European 
 Cultural Foundation is opening its historical archives for a Creative Rese
 arch Fellowship: one professional creative practitioner will research ECF'
 s lesser-known role in shaping the ERASMUS student mobility programme (fro
 m the European Institute of Education and the Office for Cooperation in Ed
 ucation to the ERASMUS Bureau\, which ran the scheme until 1994) and trans
 late that history into a public-facing artwork about what European belongi
 ng feels like and what the next ERASMUS could be. WHAT YOU GET: a EUR 10\,
 000 grant for travel\, living costs and production\; a research residency 
 at the Historical Archives of the European Union in Florence during the fi
 rst trimester of 2027\; and presentation of the finished work in Brussels 
 in September 2027\, in partnership with the House of European History. WHO
 : creative practitioners residing in a Council of Europe member state with
  a track record of research-based work for museum contexts. DEADLINE: 1 De
 cember 2026\, 17:00 CET. The full call is linked from ECF's open calls and
  social channels\; a dedicated call page could not be located at the time 
 of adding.\n\nOrganisation: European Cultural Foundation (ECF)\, with the 
 Historical Archives of the European Union (EUI) and the House of European 
 History\nLocation: Research stay in Florence\, Italy (Historical Archives 
 of the EU) in the first trimester of 2027\; final work presented in Brusse
 ls in September 2027. Open to creative practitioners residing in a Council
  of Europe member state.\nAward: EUR 10\,000 grant covering travel\, livin
 g expenses and artistic production. One fellowship.\nDuration: Research re
 sidency in Florence in the first trimester of 2027 (January to March)\; pr
 esentation in September 2027\n\nMore: https://culturalfoundation.eu/open-c
 alls/?utm_source=artificialnouveaugrantdesk&utm_medium=referral
URL:https://culturalfoundation.eu/open-calls/?utm_source=artificialnouveaug
 rantdesk&utm_medium=referral
LOCATION:Research stay in Florence\, Italy (Historical Archives of the EU) 
 in the first trimester of 2027\; final work presented in Brussels in Septe
 mber 2027. Open to creative practitioners residing in a Council of Europe 
 member state.
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:camargo-fellowship-2027-2028@artificialnouveau.github.io
DTSTAMP:20261104T144744Z
DTSTART;VALUE=DATE:20261001
DTEND;VALUE=DATE:20261002
SUMMARY:Deadline: Camargo Fellowship 2027-2028 for Artists\, Scholars and T
 hinkers (Cassis\, France)
DESCRIPTION:The Camargo Foundation invites artists\, scholars and thinkers 
 to its flagship Core Fellowship: a ten-week self-directed residency on its
  clifftop campus overlooking the Mediterranean in Cassis\, France\, dedica
 ted to research\, experimentation and interdisciplinary exchange. Curators
  apply under the Thinkers category\, which covers accomplished professiona
 ls and practitioners in cultural and creative fields who are professionall
 y engaged in critical thought\, including curators\, critics and journalis
 ts\; projects should engage theoretical\, artistic or societal questions. 
 WHAT YOU GET: a stipend of EUR 350 per week (EUR 3\,500 total)\, basic tra
 nsportation funding\, and a furnished apartment with an equipped kitchen o
 n campus. Fourteen fellowships are awarded per year: seven for artists and
  seven for scholars and thinkers. RESIDENCY PERIODS: Fall 2027 (8 Septembe
 r to 17 November 2027) or Spring 2028 (23 February to 4 May 2028). ELIGIBI
 LITY: artists in visual arts\, performance\, writing\, film\, music or mul
 tidisciplinary work with at least 5 years of professional practice\; schol
 ars holding or pursuing a PhD focused on French and Francophone cultures o
 r cross-cultural Mediterranean studies\; and thinkers as described above. 
 APPLICATION: via Submittable with a 100-word proposal summary\, a 1\,200-w
 ord proposal narrative\, a 300-word rationale for Camargo\, a 300-word car
 eer-stage rationale\, a CV (max 3 pages)\, work samples and two references
 . NOTE: the foundation has announced that this is the final Fellowship sel
 ected through a public open call until further notice\; a new selection pr
 ocess begins in July 2027\, making this the last chance to apply directly.
 \n\nOrganisation: Camargo Foundation\nLocation: Camargo Foundation campus\
 , Cassis\, France. Open worldwide.\nAward: EUR 350 per week (EUR 3\,500 to
 tal for the ten-week residency)\, plus basic travel funding and a furnishe
 d apartment with equipped kitchen on the Camargo campus. 14 fellowships pe
 r year: 7 for artists and 7 for scholars and thinkers. No application fee.
 \nDuration: Ten weeks\, either 8 September to 17 November 2027 or 23 Febru
 ary to 4 May 2028.\n\nMore: https://camargofoundation.org/en/camargo-fello
 wship-2026-2027?utm_source=artificialnouveaugrantdesk&utm_medium=referral
URL:https://camargofoundation.org/en/camargo-fellowship-2026-2027?utm_sourc
 e=artificialnouveaugrantdesk&utm_medium=referral
LOCATION:Camargo Foundation campus\, Cassis\, France. Open worldwide.
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:braunschweig-projects-curatorial-fellowship-2027-28@artificialnouveau.g
 ithub.io
DTSTAMP:20261104T144744Z
DTSTART;VALUE=DATE:20261004
DTEND;VALUE=DATE:20261005
SUMMARY:Deadline: Braunschweig Projects 2027/28: Curatorial Fellowship at H
 BK Braunschweig
DESCRIPTION:The curatorial strand of the Braunschweig Projects fellowship p
 rogramme at Braunschweig University of Art (HBK Braunschweig)\, funded by 
 the State of Lower Saxony\, offers one curator a twelve-month fellowship b
 eginning 1 April 2027. The fellow develops a curatorial concept culminatin
 g in a final exhibition and presentation in January/February 2028\; projec
 ts should ideally connect to a museum\, collection or cultural institution
  in Lower Saxony. WHAT YOU GET: EUR 1\,600 per month for twelve months\, a
  rent-free studio apartment designed for one person (with a EUR 250 monthl
 y contribution towards incidental living costs charged to the fellow)\, a 
 subsidy of up to EUR 4\,000 to support exhibition activities and a EUR 2\,
 000 travel budget. ELIGIBILITY: applicants must have gained initial curato
 rial experience of at least three years\; no formal degree is specified. F
 ellows may not hold regular income from third parties or other state fundi
 ng during the fellowship\, and must spend at least eight months of the HBK
  lecture period on site in Braunschweig. Participation in the Welcome Week
 s (19 to 30 April 2027) and the final presentation is mandatory. APPLICATI
 ON: submit a project draft covering research\, preparation\, implementatio
 n\, budget and communication plans via the HBK application portal. DEADLIN
 E: 4 October 2026\, 23:59. The artistic fellowships strand (fine arts and 
 sound art) runs in parallel with the same deadline.\n\nOrganisation: Braun
 schweig University of Art (HBK Braunschweig)\, on behalf of the State of L
 ower Saxony\nLocation: Braunschweig\, Germany. Open worldwide\; at least e
 ight months of the HBK lecture period must be spent on site.\nAward: Stipe
 nd of EUR 1\,600 per month for 12 months (EUR 19\,200 total)\, plus a rent
 -free one-person studio apartment in Braunschweig (the fellow pays EUR 250
  per month towards incidental living costs)\, a subsidy of up to EUR 4\,00
 0 for exhibition activities and a EUR 2\,000 travel budget. No application
  fee.\nDuration: Twelve months from 1 April 2027\, with a final presentati
 on in January/February 2028.\n\nMore: https://www.hbk-bs.de/hochschule/sti
 pendienprogramme/braunschweig-projects-kuratorinnen-stipendium/?utm_source
 =artificialnouveaugrantdesk&utm_medium=referral
URL:https://www.hbk-bs.de/hochschule/stipendienprogramme/braunschweig-proje
 cts-kuratorinnen-stipendium/?utm_source=artificialnouveaugrantdesk&utm_med
 ium=referral
LOCATION:Braunschweig\, Germany. Open worldwide\; at least eight months of 
 the HBK lecture period must be spent on site.
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:khk-cure-fellowships-2027-2028@artificialnouveau.github.io
DTSTAMP:20261104T144744Z
DTSTART;VALUE=DATE:20261130
DTEND;VALUE=DATE:20261201
SUMMARY:Deadline: KHK CURE Fellowships 2027/2028\, Saarland University (the
 me: Things)
DESCRIPTION:The Kaete Hamburger Centre for Cultural Practices of Reparation
  (KHK CURE) at Saarland University invites applications for up to twelve f
 ellowships for its fourth year\, October 2027 to September 2028. CURE welc
 omes scholars from cultural studies\, the humanities and the social scienc
 es whose work examines questions of reparation and irreparability\; artist
 s holding a doctorate and experienced in academic collaboration may also a
 pply. ANNUAL THEME: 'Things'\, foregrounding the material dimension of rep
 aration: objects\, artefacts and material environments through which pract
 ices such as repair\, mending and reuse acquire cultural and political sig
 nificance. Proposals must align with at least one of the Centre's three th
 ematic fields (history\, experience or nature) and address the annual them
 e. CONDITIONS: fellowships run six to twelve months from 1 October 2027\; 
 fellows on unpaid leave receive approximately EUR 6\,100 to 7\,200 per mon
 th before taxes depending on qualifications\, or the home institution is r
 eimbursed for replacement teaching\; one round trip to Saarbruecken is rei
 mbursed under the Saarland Travel Expense Act\; a fully equipped individua
 l workspace is provided and an apartment can be arranged for around EUR 1\
 ,250 per month\; fellows must live in Saarbruecken and take part in the Ce
 ntre's international\, interdisciplinary research community. ELIGIBILITY: 
 a completed PhD (submitted and defended) by the deadline\; retired persons
  and Saarland University staff are not eligible. HOW TO APPLY: via the onl
 ine form with a one-page motivation letter\, CV (max 3 pages)\, list of pu
 blications\, and a five-page project description with bibliography\, in En
 glish\, German or French. Notification by 30 April 2027. Deadline 30 Novem
 ber 2026.\n\nOrganisation: Kaete Hamburger Centre for Cultural Practices o
 f Reparation (CURE)\, Saarland University\nLocation: Saarbruecken\, German
 y. Fellows must live in Saarbruecken during the fellowship and participate
  actively in centre events\; extended research absences are not permitted.
  Open internationally\; retired persons and Saarland University staff are 
 not eligible.\nAward: Up to twelve fellowships. Fellows on unpaid leave fr
 om their institution receive approximately EUR 6\,100 to 7\,200 per month 
 before taxes\, depending on qualifications\; alternatively\, fellows retai
 ning their salary can have their home institution reimbursed for replaceme
 nt teaching. One round trip to Saarbruecken is reimbursed\, a fully equipp
 ed individual workspace is provided\, and an apartment can be arranged for
  around EUR 1\,250 per month. Eligible accompanying family members may rec
 eive additional support. No application fee.\nDuration: Six to twelve mont
 hs\, beginning 1 October 2027\n\nMore: https://cure.uni-saarland.de/en/fel
 lows/call-for-applications?utm_source=artificialnouveaugrantdesk&utm_mediu
 m=referral
URL:https://cure.uni-saarland.de/en/fellows/call-for-applications?utm_sourc
 e=artificialnouveaugrantdesk&utm_medium=referral
LOCATION:Saarbruecken\, Germany. Fellows must live in Saarbruecken during t
 he fellowship and participate actively in centre events\; extended researc
 h absences are not permitted. Open internationally\; retired persons and S
 aarland University staff are not eligible.
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:american-academy-berlin-prize-2027-28@artificialnouveau.github.io
DTSTAMP:20261104T144744Z
DTSTART;VALUE=DATE:20260925
DTEND;VALUE=DATE:20260926
SUMMARY:Deadline: American Academy in Berlin: Berlin Prize Fellowship 2027-
 28
DESCRIPTION:The American Academy in Berlin is an independent\, nonprofit in
 stitute for advanced study in the arts\, humanities\, social sciences\, la
 w\, journalism and public policy\, whose primary mission is to strengthen 
 the cultural and intellectual ties between the United States and Germany. 
 Each year\, after a rigorous selection process\, the Academy awards roughl
 y two dozen semester-long Berlin Prize fellowships to individuals who demo
 nstrate excellence in their fields. DISCIPLINES: applications are welcome 
 across the humanities\, social sciences\, law\, journalism and various are
 as of public policy. The Academy generally encourages projects addressing 
 pressing issues of the day\, specifically naming democracy\, security\, te
 chnology and society (especially AI)\, energy including climate\, and glob
 al order. Dedicated fellowships also support projects in Jewish studies\, 
 German studies\, and public health and biotechnology. Project proposals ne
 ed not focus on Germany\, but candidates should explain how a residency in
  Berlin would enhance their work. NOT ACCEPTED: proposals in mathematics o
 r the natural sciences. NOTE FOR ARTISTS: visual artists and composers are
  invited through separate\, nomination-based competitions\, so there is no
  open application route for them here. ELIGIBILITY: fellowships are restri
 cted to citizens and permanent residents of the United States. Both emergi
 ng and established figures are eligible. PhD holders qualify as emerging s
 cholars with a strong record of peer-reviewed work beyond the dissertation
 \; candidates in non-academic fields such as journalism\, filmmaking and p
 olicy need a significant record of publication or production\; fiction and
  nonfiction writers must have published at least one book with a reputable
  press. WHAT FELLOWS DO: fellows present their work to audiences in and ar
 ound Berlin through lectures\, readings\, public discussions\, performance
 s and film screenings\, and should be willing to engage with relevant Germ
 an institutions\, organizations and the media\; these activities are integ
 ral to the Academy's mission of fostering transatlantic dialogue. APPLICAT
 ION MATERIALS: a project proposal of 1\,500 to 1\,750 words plus a one-pag
 e bibliography\; a curriculum vitae of up to 10 pages\; two writing sample
 s totalling no more than 60 pages\; and three letters of recommendation\, 
 which are due 2 October 2026. DEADLINE: Friday 25 September 2026 at 12:00 
 noon Eastern Time (18:00 Central European Time). TIMELINE: all candidates 
 are notified of the competition outcome by email in late March 2027\; the 
 2027-28 Berlin Prize recipients are announced in May 2027.\n\nOrganisation
 : American Academy in Berlin\nLocation: Berlin\, Germany\, at the Hans Arn
 hold Center. RESTRICTED: fellowships are open only to citizens and permane
 nt residents of the United States.\nAward: Monthly stipend of USD 5\,000\,
  plus roundtrip airfare\, accommodation at or near the Hans Arnhold Center
 \, partial board\, in-house library services and professional networking o
 pportunities. Roughly two dozen fellowships awarded each year. No applicat
 ion fee.\nDuration: One full academic semester (about 4 months). Fall runs
  mid-August to mid-December\; spring runs late January to late May.\n\nMor
 e: https://www.americanacademy.de/apply/apply-for-a-fellowship/?utm_source
 =artificialnouveaugrantdesk&utm_medium=referral
URL:https://www.americanacademy.de/apply/apply-for-a-fellowship/?utm_source
 =artificialnouveaugrantdesk&utm_medium=referral
LOCATION:Berlin\, Germany\, at the Hans Arnhold Center. RESTRICTED: fellows
 hips are open only to citizens and permanent residents of the United State
 s.
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:msca-postdoctoral-fellowships-2026@artificialnouveau.github.io
DTSTAMP:20261104T144744Z
DTSTART;VALUE=DATE:20260909
DTEND;VALUE=DATE:20260910
SUMMARY:Deadline: Marie Skłodowska-Curie Postdoctoral Fellowships 2026 (MS
 CA-PF)
DESCRIPTION:MSCA Postdoctoral Fellowships support researchers holding a PhD
  who wish to carry out research abroad\, acquire new skills\, develop thei
 r careers and have international mobility. Open to excellent researchers o
 f any nationality. The 2026 call opened 9 April 2026 and closes 9 Septembe
 r 2026 at 17:00 CEST\; notification of call results expected February 2027
 \; grant agreement signature April 2027. Two strands: European Postdoctora
 l Fellowships (12 to 24 months in Europe) and Global Postdoctoral Fellowsh
 ips (12 to 24 months outside Europe + 12-month return phase in Europe). Th
 e project must take place in a country different from where the researcher
  has worked or studied. Approximately 1\,600 projects funded. Apply via th
 e EU Funding & Tenders Opportunities Portal\; submission is by the fellow 
 plus host institution. Eligibility: researcher must have a PhD at the call
  deadline (or have submitted thesis with all requirements met)\, maximum 8
  years full-time-equivalent research experience post-PhD\, must comply wit
 h the mobility rule (no more than 12 months in the host country in the 36 
 months before the deadline).\n\nOrganisation: European Commission (Marie S
 kłodowska-Curie Actions\, under Horizon Europe\; managed by the European 
 Research Executive Agency\, REA)\nLocation: Europe (host institution in an
  EU Member State or Horizon Europe Associated Country\; also possible: Glo
 bal Postdoctoral Fellowships hosted in non-associated third countries with
  a return phase in Europe)\nAward: Standard MSCA unit costs covering resea
 rcher allowance (living\, mobility\, family)\, research/training/networkin
 g costs and management/indirect costs (annual values published in the call
 's Work Programme\; typically a fully-funded postdoc package of roughly 
 €5\,000 to €8\,000+ per researcher per month equivalent depending on h
 ost country correction coefficient)\nDuration: 12 to 24 months (European P
 ostdoctoral Fellowships)\; Global Postdoctoral Fellowships are 24 to 36 mo
 nths including a 12-month return phase in Europe\n\nMore: https://marie-sk
 lodowska-curie-actions.ec.europa.eu/funding/msca-postdoctoral-fellowships-
 2026?utm_source=artificialnouveaugrantdesk&utm_medium=referral
URL:https://marie-sklodowska-curie-actions.ec.europa.eu/funding/msca-postdo
 ctoral-fellowships-2026?utm_source=artificialnouveaugrantdesk&utm_medium=r
 eferral
LOCATION:Europe (host institution in an EU Member State or Horizon Europe A
 ssociated Country\; also possible: Global Postdoctoral Fellowships hosted 
 in non-associated third countries with a return phase in Europe)
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:canon-foundation-europe-research-fellowships-2026@artificialnouveau.git
 hub.io
DTSTAMP:20261104T144744Z
DTSTART;VALUE=DATE:20260915
DTEND;VALUE=DATE:20260916
SUMMARY:Deadline: Canon Foundation in Europe Research Fellowships 2026 (Eur
 ope–Japan Exchange)
DESCRIPTION:Canon Foundation in Europe Research Fellowships support bilater
 al research exchange between Europe and Japan\, awarding up to 15 fellowsh
 ips annually. AMOUNT: up to EUR 30\,000 per year (pro-rated for shorter st
 ays)\, flexible — covers living costs\, travel\, insurance\, research ex
 penses\, and books\; fellows determine their own allocation. ELIGIBILITY: 
 European nationals (including UK\, Israel\, Switzerland\, Turkey\, Balkan 
 and Baltic countries) conducting research in Japan\; or Japanese nationals
  conducting research in Europe. Applicants must have completed at least a 
 Master's or PhD degree within the last 10 years (older qualifications may 
 apply with supporting documentation). No restrictions on research field. P
 riority given to first-time applicants to Europe/Japan. Fellows arrange th
 eir own host institution and advisor. Up to 15 fellowships awarded annuall
 y. Deadline: 15 September 2026\, 23:59 CET (for fellowships starting Janua
 ry-December 2027). Apply at https://portal.canonfoundation.org/. Contact: 
 canon.foundation@canon-europe.com.\n\nOrganisation: Canon Foundation in Eu
 rope\nLocation: Europe or Japan (bilateral exchange\; fellows arrange own 
 host institution)\nAward: Up to EUR 30\,000 per year (pro-rated for shorte
 r stays). No application fee.\nDuration: Minimum 3 months\, maximum 1 year
 . Fellowships for 2027 start.\n\nMore: https://portal.canonfoundation.org/
 ?utm_source=artificialnouveaugrantdesk&utm_medium=referral
URL:https://portal.canonfoundation.org/?utm_source=artificialnouveaugrantde
 sk&utm_medium=referral
LOCATION:Europe or Japan (bilateral exchange\; fellows arrange own host ins
 titution)
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:kb-researcher-in-residence-2027@artificialnouveau.github.io
DTSTAMP:20261104T144744Z
DTSTART;VALUE=DATE:20260927
DTEND;VALUE=DATE:20260928
SUMMARY:Deadline: KB Researcher-in-Residence (RiR) 2027
DESCRIPTION:ELIGIBILITY: two Researcher-in-Residence positions in 2027. Ope
 n to early-career researchers of any discipline (data science\, social sci
 ence\, computer science\, humanities) who are currently working on a PhD o
 r within 5 years of their PhD end date. You must have\, or be able to secu
 re\, an affiliation with a Dutch university or research institute willing 
 to second you\; external PhD students may use the funding to obtain such a
  contract. Your project must make use of one or more of the KB digital col
 lections and you must be able to work with primarily Dutch data. You must 
 be available at 0.5 FTE over 6 months and able to spend two days a week on
 -site at the KB in The Hague. COLLECTIONS: over 130 million digitized page
 s of newspapers\, magazines\, radio bulletins and books from 1470 onward (
 via Delpher and DBNL)\, a born-digital web collection of more than 23\,000
  websites\, ground-truth and derived datasets on the KB Lab\, and metadata
  including Linked Data. APPLY: use the official proposal template and subm
 it a single PDF by 27 September 2026 to dh@kb.nl\, after reading the call'
 s terms and conditions\; questions to dh@kb.nl. TIMELINE: deadline 27 Sept
 ember 2026\; internal eligibility review followed by an external committee
  review\; outcome notified November 2026\; residencies start Q1 and Q3 202
 7. DISCIPLINES: digital humanities / data science / computational research
  with cultural-heritage collections. NOTE: the output is usually a dataset
  or tool prototype hosted on the KB Lab\; you agree to share results via b
 log posts on the KB Lab and to publish resulting tools or data under an op
 en license within 6 months of the residency. Proposals should align with t
 he KB Research Agenda or the Cultural AI Lab.\n\nOrganisation: KB\, Nation
 al Library of the Netherlands\nLocation: The Hague\, Netherlands (two days
  per week on-site at the KB premises)\nAward: Secondment at 0.5 FTE for 6 
 months based on your current salary\, up to EUR 5\,590 gross per month for
  a full-time-equivalent position\, plus travel expenses\, office facilitie
 s\, and support from a research software engineer\, research advisor\, and
  collection and data specialists. No application fee.\nDuration: 6 months 
 at 0.5 FTE (two days per week on-site)\; two positions in 2027\, starting 
 Q1 and Q3\n\nMore: https://lab.kb.nl/news/call-kb-researcher-residence-rir
 -2027?utm_source=artificialnouveaugrantdesk&utm_medium=referral
URL:https://lab.kb.nl/news/call-kb-researcher-residence-rir-2027?utm_source
 =artificialnouveaugrantdesk&utm_medium=referral
LOCATION:The Hague\, Netherlands (two days per week on-site at the KB premi
 ses)
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:columbia-ideas-imagination-paris-2027-28@artificialnouveau.github.io
DTSTAMP:20261104T144744Z
DTSTART;VALUE=DATE:20260930
DTEND;VALUE=DATE:20261001
SUMMARY:Deadline: Columbia Institute for Ideas and Imagination: Paris Fello
 wship 2027-28
DESCRIPTION:Columbia University's Institute for Ideas and Imagination bring
 s a cohort of scholars and artists to Reid Hall in Paris for an academic y
 ear (September 2027 to May 2028)\, with a USD 75\,000 stipend\, housing\, 
 and research support. The creative-arts category explicitly includes visua
 l and sound arts and film alongside writing and composition. ELIGIBILITY: 
 any nationality\, but artists must not be permanently residentent in the U
 nited States (Netherlands-based artists qualify). Deadline 30 September 20
 26.\n\nOrganisation: Columbia University Institute for Ideas and Imaginati
 on\nLocation: Paris\, France (Reid Hall). Any nationality\; artists must N
 OT be permanently resident in the United States.\nAward: USD 75\,000 stipe
 nd\, plus housing at Reid Hall in Paris and office and research support\, 
 September 2027 to May 2028. No application fee.\nDuration: Fellowship year
  September 2027 to May 2028 at Reid Hall\, Paris.\n\nMore: https://ideasim
 agination.columbia.edu/apply/applying-for-a-fellowship/?utm_source=artific
 ialnouveaugrantdesk&utm_medium=referral
URL:https://ideasimagination.columbia.edu/apply/applying-for-a-fellowship/?
 utm_source=artificialnouveaugrantdesk&utm_medium=referral
LOCATION:Paris\, France (Reid Hall). Any nationality\; artists must NOT be 
 permanently resident in the United States.
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:ecf-artistic-research-fellowship-2026@artificialnouveau.github.io
DTSTAMP:20261104T144744Z
DTSTART;VALUE=DATE:20260930
DTEND;VALUE=DATE:20261001
SUMMARY:Deadline: European Cultural Foundation: Artistic Research Fellowshi
 p (ECF Historical Archives)
DESCRIPTION:The European Cultural Foundation invites artists to research it
 s historical archives\, held at the Historical Archives of the European Un
 ion in Florence\, through a new Artistic Research Fellowship with a EUR 10
 \,000 grant covering travel\, accommodation\, and living costs\; the artis
 tic output is delivered within two years. A strong fit for archive-driven\
 , research-based\, and critical data practices. Deadline 30 September 2026
 \, 17:00 CET. ELIGIBILITY: professional creatives in visual arts\, perform
 ing arts\, film and audiovisual practices\, podcasting and audio storytell
 ing\, documentary\, journalism or literary practices\, who can demonstrate
  a research-based practice and a clear dissemination strategy. Application
 s and all supporting documentation must be in English\, submitted via the 
 online form. Fellows work at the HAEU inside the scholarly community of th
 e European University Institute\, with the archives housed at Villa Salvia
 ti. A parallel Academic Research Fellowship of EUR 5\,000\, for PhD and po
 stdoctoral researchers\, early-career researchers within ten years of thei
 r master's or doctorate\, and independent scholars\, is listed separately 
 on the desk and shares this deadline.\n\nOrganisation: European Cultural F
 oundation\nLocation: Archives held at the Historical Archives of the Europ
 ean Union\, Florence\, Italy. Open to artists able to work with the archiv
 es.\nAward: EUR 10\,000 artistic-research grant covering travel\, accommod
 ation\, and living costs\; output delivered within 2 years. No application
  fee.\nDuration: Research fellowship in the ECF historical archives\; outp
 ut within two years.\n\nMore: https://culturalfoundation.eu/open-calls/new
 -fellowship-programme-to-research-the-ecf-historical-archives/?utm_source=
 artificialnouveaugrantdesk&utm_medium=referral
URL:https://culturalfoundation.eu/open-calls/new-fellowship-programme-to-re
 search-the-ecf-historical-archives/?utm_source=artificialnouveaugrantdesk&
 utm_medium=referral
LOCATION:Archives held at the Historical Archives of the European Union\, F
 lorence\, Italy. Open to artists able to work with the archives.
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:ecf-academic-research-fellowship-2026@artificialnouveau.github.io
DTSTAMP:20261104T144744Z
DTSTART;VALUE=DATE:20260930
DTEND;VALUE=DATE:20261001
SUMMARY:Deadline: European Cultural Foundation: Academic Research Fellowshi
 p (ECF Historical Archives)
DESCRIPTION:The academic track of the ECF Fellowship Programme\, which fost
 ers new research engaging with the history of the European Cultural Founda
 tion and the broader role of culture in European integration. The ECF was 
 established in 1954 by figures including Denis de Rougemont\, Robert Schum
 an\, Hendrik Brugmans and Prince Bernhard of the Netherlands\, on the beli
 ef that Europe will not be built by treaties alone. Over seventy years it 
 has produced programmes\, institutes\, campaigns and collaborations\, incl
 uding a contribution to shaping and implementing the ERASMUS student mobil
 ity scheme. That legacy is now preserved at the Historical Archives of the
  European Union in Florence alongside the records of the EU's main institu
 tions\, a placement that itself recognises the Foundation's role in Europe
 an integration. ELIGIBILITY for the Academic Research Fellowship: research
 ers in European studies\, cultural studies\, history and related fields ac
 ross the humanities and social sciences\, specifically PhD and postdoctora
 l researchers\, early-career researchers within ten years of completing a 
 master's or doctoral degree\, and independent scholars with an active rese
 arch profile. Fellows work at the HAEU embedded in the international schol
 arly community of the European University Institute\, with the archives ho
 used at Villa Salviati. Applications and all supporting documentation must
  be submitted in English through the online form. NOTE: this is the EUR 5\
 ,000 academic track. The EUR 10\,000 Artistic Research Fellowship\, open t
 o professional creatives in visual arts\, performing arts\, film and audio
 visual practices\, podcasting and audio storytelling\, documentary\, journ
 alism and literary practices\, is listed separately on the desk and shares
  the same deadline.\n\nOrganisation: European Cultural Foundation\, hosted
  at the Historical Archives of the European Union\nLocation: Historical Ar
 chives of the European Union\, Villa Salviati\, Florence\, Italy\, within 
 the European University Institute.\nAward: EUR 5\,000 grant to support a r
 esearch stay at the HAEU\, covering travel\, accommodation and living expe
 nses. The parallel Artistic Research Fellowship carries EUR 10\,000 and ad
 ditionally covers artistic production. No application fee.\nDuration: Rese
 arch stay at the HAEU. Outcomes must be completed and publicly presented o
 r published within two years of signing the Grant Agreement. Deadline 30 S
 eptember 2026 at 17:00 CET.\n\nMore: https://culturalfoundation.eu/open-ca
 lls/new-fellowship-programme-to-research-the-ecf-historical-archives/?utm_
 source=artificialnouveaugrantdesk&utm_medium=referral
URL:https://culturalfoundation.eu/open-calls/new-fellowship-programme-to-re
 search-the-ecf-historical-archives/?utm_source=artificialnouveaugrantdesk&
 utm_medium=referral
LOCATION:Historical Archives of the European Union\, Villa Salviati\, Flore
 nce\, Italy\, within the European University Institute.
END:VEVENT
